-- Formal semantics of the Java fragment: a functional big-step evaluator, in
-- Arend.
--
-- `JavaAst.ard` defines the SYNTAX of the fragment we commit to generating and
-- says of itself that it "is the object a future operational semantics (and
-- hence any correctness statement about the backend) will be given for". This
-- module is that semantics. Nothing in the compiler imports it: it is a
-- specification, not a pass.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHY A FUNCTION AND NOT A RELATION (the one place this file differs in style
-- from `LambdaBoxEval.ard`, deliberately)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `LambdaBoxEval.ard` is a `\data ... \elim` relation because it TRANSCRIBES a
-- relation, MetaRocq's `EWcbvEval.eval`. There the upstream is the oracle.
--
-- Here there is no upstream to transcribe -- no existing formalisation is the
-- semantics of THIS fragment. So the two oracles have to be manufactured, and
-- both of them want a function:
--
--   1. a JLS citation per rule, in place of an upstream rule name;
--   2. an EXECUTABLE evaluator, so the semantics can be differential-tested
--      against `javac` + `java` on the corpus. A relation cannot be run.
--
-- This is Owens-Myreen-Kumar-Tan's *functional big-step* style (ESOP 2016), the
-- style the CakeML verified compiler is written in: a recursive interpreter made
-- total by a decreasing clock, with a timeout result, where divergence is
-- "times out for every clock". Their claim -- and the reason this is not a
-- compromise -- is that the resulting induction principle is BETTER for
-- compiler proofs than a relation's, not worse.
--
-- Determinism costs nothing here: Java fixes evaluation order left to right
-- (JLS 15.7), unlike C, which is why none of `Csem.v`'s reduction-context
-- machinery is needed. `JEval` at the bottom recovers a relation from the
-- function, for the theorem statement.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT IS AND IS NOT MODELLED
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Modelled: every `JExpr`, `JStmt`, `JType`, `JMember` constructor of
-- `JavaAst.ard`. The fragment is closed by construction -- the generator can
-- only emit what those constructors allow -- so "unmodelled Java features"
-- cannot appear in the input to this function. What they CAN affect is the two
-- things outside it, and both are named:
--
--   * `JavaPrint.ard` (138 lines) turns this AST into text and is TRUSTED, in
--     the same sense as CompCert's `printClight.ml`. Its documented gap is that
--     `jStringLit` is not escaped.
--   * `runtime/Rt.java` (618 lines) is a parameter, `RtSpec` -- see
--     `JavaValue.ard`.
--
-- No output component. `ToJava.ard` emits `jExprStmt` exactly once, for
-- `Rt.runMain(...)` (`ToJava.ard:386`), and never emits `System.out.println` --
-- checked, not assumed. The observable of a generated program is therefore the
-- VALUE returned by `__main()`, which `Rt.runMain` prints; rendering it as text
-- to compare with `java`'s stdout belongs outside this module.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- KNOWN GAPS, stated rather than hidden
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--   * `new java.math.BigInteger("<digits>")` is `rStuck`: it needs a decimal
--     parser, and it only arises under the non-default `targetBigInteger`
--     (`ToJava.ard:118`); `targetLong` is what the harness runs.
--   * Array index out of bounds and a null receiver are modelled as thrown
--     exceptions (JLS 10.4, 15.12.4.4), but arithmetic overflow inside `Rt` is
--     the instance's business, not this file's.

\import JavaAst
\import JavaValue
\import Data.Bool
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String
\import Set

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Class-level lookup
--
-- One flat method table over the single generated class, as every comparable
-- formalisation does it (Featherweight Java's `CT`, Jinja's `class P C`,
-- Clight's `prog_defs`, Malfunction's global bindings).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func lookupMethodIn (ms : Array JMember) (nm : JIdent) : Maybe JMethod \elim ms
  | nil => nothing
  | jMemberComment _ :: ms => lookupMethodIn ms nm
  | jMethod m :: ms => \case m.mName == nm \with {
    | true => just m
    | false => lookupMethodIn ms nm
  }

\func lookupMethod (cls : JClass) (nm : JIdent) : Maybe JMethod =>
  lookupMethodIn cls.classMembers nm

-- Bind a method's parameters to the actual arguments, positionally.
\func bindParams (ps : Array JParam) (vs : Array Value) : Maybe Env \elim ps, vs
  | nil, nil => just nil
  | nil, _ :: _ => nothing
  | _ :: _, nil => nothing
  | p :: ps, v :: vs => \case bindParams ps vs \with {
    | nothing => nothing
    | just env => just ((p.paramName, v) :: env)
  }

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Exceptions the semantics itself raises
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func excCCE : Value => vExcObj "java.lang.ClassCastException" "cast failed"
\func excNPE : Value => vExcObj "java.lang.NullPointerException" "null receiver"
\func excAIOOBE : Value => vExcObj "java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException" "index"

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Casts (JLS 5.5, 15.16)
--
-- Every value in the fragment is statically `Object`, so a cast is a runtime
-- type test. Two details that matter and are easy to get wrong:
--   * casting `null` ALWAYS succeeds, whatever the target type;
--   * a failing cast throws `ClassCastException`, it does not get stuck.
-- Only the class names the generator actually casts to are recognised:
-- `Rt.Fn` (before `.apply`) and `Rt.Data` (before `.tag`/`.fields`).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func castOk (t : JType) (v : Value) : Bool \elim t
  | tObject => true
  | tVoid => false
  | tArray _ => \case v \with {
    | vArr _ => true
    | vNull => true
    | _ => false
  }
  | tClass q => \case v \with {
    | vNull => true
    | vClos _ _ _ _ => q == "Rt.Fn"
    | vBuiltin _ _ => q == "Rt.Fn"
    | vData _ _ => q == "Rt.Data"
    | vStr _ => q == "String"
    | vLong _ => q == "Long"
    | vBigInt _ => q == "java.math.BigInteger"
    | vExcObj c _ => c == q
    | _ => false
  }

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The evaluator.
--
-- Fuel (`n`) is the first `\elim` argument and strictly decreases at every
-- recursive call, in every direction of the mutual cycle, so termination is
-- structural on it. `evalArgs` exists because a store must be threaded from one
-- element of an argument array to the next, which `mkArray` cannot do -- so
-- `ToJava.ard`'s `xs i`-inside-`mkArray` discipline does not transfer here.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func evalExpr (rt : RtSpec) (cls : JClass) (n : Nat) (st : Store) (env : Env) (e : JExpr)
  : Res (\Sigma Store ExprOut) \elim n, e
  | 0, _ => rOutOfFuel

  -- Literals. JLS 3.10.
  | suc _, jStringLit s => rOk (st, eVal (vStr s))
  | suc _, jIntLit k => rOk (st, eVal (vInt k))
  | suc _, jLongLit i => rOk (st, eVal (vLong i))

  -- A local or parameter. Reading a declared-but-unassigned local is `rStuck`
  -- (JLS 16 definite assignment: `javac` rejects such a program).
  | suc _, jVar x => \case lookupVar env x \with {
    | nothing => rStuck
    | just vUninit => rStuck
    | just v => rOk (st, eVal v)
  }

  -- `Rt` as a receiver. `ToJava.ard` only ever emits `jStatic runtimeClass`.
  | suc _, jStatic q => rOk (st, eVal (vStaticRef q))

  -- A closure. The environment is captured BY VALUE, now (JLS 15.9.5, 4.12.4).
  -- This is the rule the `fix` knot depends on: what gets captured is a
  -- reference to an array whose slots are still empty, so the assignments that
  -- follow are visible through it.
  | suc _, jAnonFn iface p body => rOk (st, eVal (vClos iface p body env))

  -- `e.f`. Either a field of `Rt` (`Rt.BOX`, `Rt.PRIM_ADD_LONG`) or one of
  -- `Rt.Data`'s two fields.
  | suc n, jField recv f =>
    bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env recv) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | eExc ev => rOk (p.1, eExc ev)
      | eVal (vStaticRef _) => \case rtField {rt} f \with {
        | nothing => rStuck                       -- an Rt member no instance covers
        | just v => rOk (p.1, eVal v)
      }
      | eVal (vData tg fs) => \case f == "tag" \with {
        | true => rOk (p.1, eVal (vInt tg))
        | false => \case f == "fields" \with {
          | true => rOk (p.1, eVal (vArr fs))
          | false => rStuck
        }
      }
      | eVal vNull => rOk (p.1, eExc excNPE)
      | eVal _ => rStuck
    })

  -- `a[i]`. JLS 10.4: an out-of-range index throws.
  | suc n, jIndex a i =>
    bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env a) (\lam pa => \case pa.2 \with {
      | eExc ev => rOk (pa.1, eExc ev)
      | eVal vNull => rOk (pa.1, eExc excNPE)
      | eVal (vArr l) =>
        bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n pa.1 env i) (\lam pi => \case pi.2 \with {
          | eExc ev => rOk (pi.1, eExc ev)
          | eVal (vInt k) => \case storeGet pi.1 l \with {
            | nothing => rStuck
            | just cells => \case nthArr k cells \with {
              | nothing => rOk (pi.1, eExc excAIOOBE)
              | just v => rOk (pi.1, eVal v)
            }
          }
          | eVal _ => rStuck
        })
      | eVal _ => rStuck
    })

  -- `(T)(e)`. JLS 5.5.
  | suc n, jCast t sub =>
    bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env sub) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | eExc ev => rOk (p.1, eExc ev)
      | eVal v => \case castOk t v \with {
        | true => rOk (p.1, eVal v)
        | false => rOk (p.1, eExc excCCE)
      }
    })

  -- `recv.m(args)`. In this fragment `m` is always `apply` with one argument
  -- (`ToJava.ard` compiles every λ□ application to one `.apply`), but the rule
  -- is written for the general shape and rejects anything else.
  | suc n, jInvoke recv m args =>
    bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env recv) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | eExc ev => rOk (p.1, eExc ev)
      | eVal rv =>
        bindRes (evalArgs rt cls n p.1 env args) (\lam q => \case q.2 \with {
          | aExc ev => rOk (q.1, eExc ev)
          | aVals vs => \case m == "apply" \with {
            | false => rStuck
            | true => \case vs \with {
              | a :: nil => applyVal rt cls n q.1 rv a
              | _ => rStuck
            }
          }
        })
    })

  -- `m(args)` with `m` possibly qualified: a generated constant `c_foo()`, or
  -- one of the three `Rt` statics the generator calls.
  | suc n, jCallStatic q args =>
    bindRes (evalArgs rt cls n st env args) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | aExc ev => rOk (p.1, eExc ev)
      | aVals vs => callStatic rt cls n p.1 q vs
    })

  -- `new T(args)`.
  | suc n, jNew t args =>
    bindRes (evalArgs rt cls n st env args) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | aExc ev => rOk (p.1, eExc ev)
      | aVals vs => newObject p.1 t vs
    })

  -- `new T[]{ es }` -- a fresh array holding the element values.
  | suc n, jNewArrayInit _ es =>
    bindRes (evalArgs rt cls n st env es) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | aExc ev => rOk (p.1, eExc ev)
      | aVals vs => \case storeAlloc p.1 vs \with {
        | (st', l) => rOk (st', eVal (vArr l))
      }
    })

  -- `new T[k]` -- k cells, null-filled (JLS 10.6: reference arrays are
  -- zero-initialised). This is the `fix` knot's array, filled by `jAssign`
  -- immediately afterwards. `vNull` and not `vUninit`: definite assignment does
  -- not apply to array elements, so reading one is legal and yields null.
  | suc n, jNewArraySized _ sz =>
    bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env sz) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | eExc ev => rOk (p.1, eExc ev)
      | eVal (vInt k) => \case storeAlloc p.1 (replicateArr k vNull) \with {
        | (st', l) => rOk (st', eVal (vArr l))
      }
      | eVal _ => rStuck
    })

-- Left to right, short-circuiting on the first throw (JLS 15.7, 15.12.4.2).
\func evalArgs (rt : RtSpec) (cls : JClass) (n : Nat) (st : Store) (env : Env) (es : Array JExpr)
  : Res (\Sigma Store ArgsOut) \elim n, es
  | 0, _ => rOutOfFuel
  | suc _, nil => rOk (st, aVals nil)
  | suc n, e :: es =>
    bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env e) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | eExc ev => rOk (p.1, aExc ev)
      | eVal v =>
        bindRes (evalArgs rt cls n p.1 env es) (\lam q => \case q.2 \with {
          | aExc ev => rOk (q.1, aExc ev)
          | aVals vs => rOk (q.1, aVals (v :: vs))
        })
    })

-- `recv.apply(a)`: the fragment's only virtual call.
--
-- A closure runs its body in the captured environment extended with the
-- parameter. A builtin accumulates until it is saturated and then computes --
-- the arity discipline `Rt.curry` got wrong. Applying `null` throws
-- (JLS 15.12.4.4).
\func applyVal (rt : RtSpec) (cls : JClass) (n : Nat) (st : Store) (f a : Value)
  : Res (\Sigma Store ExprOut) \elim n
  | 0 => rOutOfFuel
  | suc n => \case f \with {
    | vNull => rOk (st, eExc excNPE)
    | vClos _ p body cenv =>
      bindRes (execStmts rt cls n st ((p, a) :: cenv) body) (\lam q => \case q.2 \with {
        | oReturn v => rOk (q.1, eVal v)
        | oExcS ev => rOk (q.1, eExc ev)
        -- `apply` is declared to return `Object`, so falling off the end is not
        -- a well-formed method body (JLS 8.4.7); cf. ClightBigstep's
        -- `outcome_result_value`.
        | oNormal _ => rStuck
      })
    | vBuiltin nm got => \case builtinArity {rt} nm \with {
      | nothing => rStuck
      | just k => \case snocArr got a \with {
        | got' => \case natEq got'.len k \with {
          | false => rOk (st, eVal (vBuiltin nm got'))
          | true => bindRes (builtinRun {rt} nm got') (\lam v => rOk (st, eVal v))
        }
      }
    }
    | _ => rStuck
  }

-- `q(args)`. Three cases: a method of the generated class; one of the `Rt`
-- statics `ToJava.ard` calls; or unknown, hence stuck.
\func callStatic (rt : RtSpec) (cls : JClass) (n : Nat) (st : Store) (q : JQIdent) (vs : Array Value)
  : Res (\Sigma Store ExprOut) \elim n
  | 0 => rOutOfFuel
  | suc n => \case lookupMethod cls q \with {
    | just m => \case bindParams m.mParams vs \with {
      | nothing => rStuck
      | just env =>
        bindRes (execStmts rt cls n st env m.mBody) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
          | oReturn v => rOk (p.1, eVal v)
          | oExcS ev => rOk (p.1, eExc ev)
          -- A `void` method may fall off the end; an `Object` one may not.
          | oNormal _ => \case m.mReturn \with {
            | tVoid => rOk (p.1, eVal vBox)
            | _ => rStuck
          }
        })
    }
    -- `Rt.unbound` / `Rt.freeVar`: ill-formed λ□ reached the generator. Both
    -- throw by construction (`Rt.java:130`, `:135`), which is the whole point
    -- of commit 729c3f7 -- they replaced a silent `null`.
    | nothing => \case q == "Rt.unbound" \with {
      | true => rOk (st, eExc (vExcObj "java.lang.IllegalStateException" "ill-formed lambda-box"))
      | false => \case q == "Rt.freeVar" \with {
        | true => rOk (st, eExc (vExcObj "java.lang.IllegalStateException" "free variable"))
        | false => \case q == "Long.valueOf" \with {
          | true => \case vs \with {
            | vLong i :: nil => rOk (st, eVal (vLong i))
            | _ => rStuck
          }
          | false => rStuck
        }
      }
    }
  }

-- `new T(args)` for the three classes the generator instantiates.
-- `java.math.BigInteger` is a named gap (see the header).
\func newObject (st : Store) (t : JType) (vs : Array Value) : Res (\Sigma Store ExprOut) =>
  \case t \with {
    | tClass q => \case q == "Rt.Data" \with {
      | true => \case vs \with {
        | vInt tg :: vArr l :: nil => rOk (st, eVal (vData tg l))
        | _ => rStuck
      }
      | false => \case q == "RuntimeException" \with {
        | true => \case vs \with {
          | vStr msg :: nil => rOk (st, eVal (vExcObj "RuntimeException" msg))
          | _ => rStuck
        }
        | false => rStuck
      }
    }
    | _ => rStuck
  }

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Statements
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func execStmt (rt : RtSpec) (cls : JClass) (n : Nat) (st : Store) (env : Env) (s : JStmt)
  : Res (\Sigma Store StmtOut) \elim n, s
  | 0, _ => rOutOfFuel

  -- Trivia. `jComment` has no effect at all -- it exists so the printer can put
  -- a comment on a line of its own.
  | suc _, jComment _ => rOk (st, oNormal env)

  -- `final Object l = e;` / `Object r;`. An uninitialised local binds
  -- `vUninit`, so reading it before assignment is stuck (JLS 16).
  | suc n, jLocal _ _ x init => \case init \with {
    | nothing => rOk (st, oNormal (bindVar env x vUninit))
    | just e =>
      bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env e) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
        | eExc ev => rOk (p.1, oExcS ev)
        | eVal v => rOk (p.1, oNormal (bindVar env x v))
      })
  }

  -- `lhs = rhs;` where `lhs` is a local or an array element. The array case is
  -- the `fix` knot and the only mutation the generator emits; the store is what
  -- makes it mean what it means.
  | suc n, jAssign lhs rhs => \case lhs \with {
    | jVar x =>
      bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env rhs) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
        | eExc ev => rOk (p.1, oExcS ev)
        | eVal v => rOk (p.1, oNormal (bindVar env x v))
      })
    | jIndex a i =>
      bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env a) (\lam pa => \case pa.2 \with {
        | eExc ev => rOk (pa.1, oExcS ev)
        | eVal vNull => rOk (pa.1, oExcS excNPE)
        | eVal (vArr l) =>
          bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n pa.1 env i) (\lam pi => \case pi.2 \with {
            | eExc ev => rOk (pi.1, oExcS ev)
            | eVal (vInt k) =>
              bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n pi.1 env rhs) (\lam pr => \case pr.2 \with {
                | eExc ev => rOk (pr.1, oExcS ev)
                | eVal v => \case storeSet pr.1 l k v \with {
                  | nothing => rOk (pr.1, oExcS excAIOOBE)
                  | just st' => rOk (st', oNormal env)
                }
              })
            | eVal _ => rStuck
          })
        | eVal _ => rStuck
      })
    | _ => rStuck
  }

  -- `throw e;`
  | suc n, jThrow e =>
    bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env e) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | eExc ev => rOk (p.1, oExcS ev)
      | eVal v => rOk (p.1, oExcS v)
    })

  -- `return e;`
  | suc n, jReturn e =>
    bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env e) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | eExc ev => rOk (p.1, oExcS ev)
      | eVal v => rOk (p.1, oReturn v)
    })

  -- An expression evaluated for its effect. The only one the generator emits is
  -- `Rt.runMain(...)`, which is not modelled, so this is here for completeness.
  | suc n, jExprStmt e =>
    bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env e) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | eExc ev => rOk (p.1, oExcS ev)
      | eVal _ => rOk (p.1, oNormal env)
    })

  -- `switch (scrut) { case t: {...} default: {...} }`. JLS 14.11.
  --
  -- The scrutinee is always `d.tag`, so an `int`. Cases are searched in order;
  -- a case body's outcome IS the switch's outcome, because the printer inserted
  -- the `break` and the fragment has no fallthrough (`JavaAst.ard:165-171`).
  -- This is where ClightBigstep's `outcome_switch` would map `Out_break` to
  -- `Out_normal`; here there is no break to map.
  | suc n, jSwitch scrut cases dflt =>
    bindRes (evalExpr rt cls n st env scrut) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | eExc ev => rOk (p.1, oExcS ev)
      | eVal (vInt tg) => \case selectCase tg cases \with {
        | just body => execStmts rt cls n p.1 env body
        | nothing => execStmts rt cls n p.1 env dflt
      }
      | eVal _ => rStuck
    })

-- First case whose label matches, in source order.
\func selectCase (tg : Nat) (cases : Array (\Sigma Nat (Array JStmt))) : Maybe (Array JStmt) \elim cases
  | nil => nothing
  | c :: cases => \case natEq c.1 tg \with {
    | true => just c.2
    | false => selectCase tg cases
  }

-- A statement sequence. An abrupt outcome stops the sequence and propagates;
-- a normal one carries its (possibly extended) environment to the next
-- statement. Falling off the end is `oNormal`.
\func execStmts (rt : RtSpec) (cls : JClass) (n : Nat) (st : Store) (env : Env) (ss : Array JStmt)
  : Res (\Sigma Store StmtOut) \elim n, ss
  | 0, _ => rOutOfFuel
  | suc _, nil => rOk (st, oNormal env)
  | suc n, s :: ss =>
    bindRes (execStmt rt cls n st env s) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
      | oReturn v => rOk (p.1, oReturn v)
      | oExcS ev => rOk (p.1, oExcS ev)
      | oNormal env' => execStmts rt cls n p.1 env' ss
    })

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Running a program
--
-- The generated class's entry point for a VALUE is `__main()`: `main(String[])`
-- only wraps it in `Rt.runMain`, which supplies the big stack and prints the
-- result. So the semantics of a generated program is the value `__main()`
-- returns -- an uncaught exception is a distinct, observable outcome, not a
-- stuck state.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\data RunOut | rvValue Value | rvThrown Value

\func runClass (rt : RtSpec) (cls : JClass) (n : Nat) : Res RunOut =>
  bindRes (callStatic rt cls n nil "__main" nil) (\lam p => \case p.2 \with {
    | eVal v => rOk (rvValue v)
    | eExc ev => rOk (rvThrown ev)
  })

-- The relation, recovered from the function: "`cls` runs to `v`" is "there is a
-- clock for which the evaluator says so". This is the right-hand side of the
-- correctness statement.
\func JEval (rt : RtSpec) (cls : JClass) (v : Value) : \Type =>
  \Sigma (n : Nat) (runClass rt cls n = rOk (rvValue v))
